iationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Vegetable Science, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Yadav RK","lastName":"Yadav","firstName":"R K","initials":"RK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Vegetable Science, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Roy A","lastName":"Roy","firstName":"Anirban","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5497-0190"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Plant Pathology, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Saxena S","lastName":"Saxena","firstName":"Shipra","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Xenesis, Absolute, Gurugram, Haryana, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Saini N","lastName":"Saini","firstName":"Navinder","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Genetics, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Jain PK","lastName":"Jain","firstName":"P K","initials":"PK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ICAR-National Institute for Plant Biotechnology, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Behera TK","lastName":"Behera","firstName":"T K","initials":"TK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ICAR-Indian Institute of Horticultural Research, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Mangal M","lastName":"Mangal","firstName":"Manisha","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Vegetable Science, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India. Electronic address: manishamangal@rediffmail.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5497-0190"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"24","journalIssueId":4218481,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Genet Eng Biotechnol","title":"Journal, genetic engineering & biotechnology","essn":"2090-5920","issn":"1687-157X","nlmid":"101317150","isoabbreviation":"J Genet Eng Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100674","abstractText":"Chilli leaf curl virus (ChiLCV), a whitefly-transmitted monopartite begomovirus, causes severe yield losses in chilli-growing regions worldwide. This study evaluated the efficiency of single and multiplex CRISPR/Cas9 gRNAs in targeting key coding regions of ChiLCV genome in Nicotiana benthamiana. Four guide RNAs (gRNAs) specific to the CP, C3/C2, C2/C1, and Rep coding regions, were designed and assembled into four single-gRNA expression constructs, each targeting one viral region at a time, and six dual-gRNA constructs, each simultaneously targeting two viral regions. Agroinfiltration assays showed that all constructs reduced viral DNA, with dual-gRNA combinations achieving stronger suppression than single gRNAs. The gRNA1 + gRNA4 dual combination, targeting CP and Rep genes, was the most effective, reducing viral titre by 49.3% at 4 dpi and 47.9% at 20 dpi in systemic leaves. Viral accumulation was quantified using qPCR, disease severity was assessed phenotypically, and gRNA/Cas9 expression was confirmed by qRT-PCR, while HRM analysis verified targeted genome modifications. Overall, the results demonstrate that dual-gRNA constructs efficiently suppress viral replication, reduce disease severity, and induce precise genome edits, underscoring the potential of multiplexed CRISPR/Cas9 for developing durable resistance against ChiLCV.","affiliation":"Division of Vegetable Science, ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"