rintPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"97","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporters form one of the largest and most functionally diverse families of membrane proteins in plants, with members of the ABCG subfamily playing central roles in the transport of specialized metabolites and stress adaptation. In legumes functional analyses of these transporters have been limited by functional redundancy, accessibility of T-DNA inserted mutant lines, and the constraints of RNA interference-based approaches.<h4>Results</h4>To address these limitations, we used Medicago truncatula ABCG46 transporter and established hairy root-based CRISPR/Cas9 genome-editing platform that enables rapid empirical validation of single guide RNAs prior to stable transformation. Two guide RNAs targeting distinct exons of MtABCG46 were evaluated in 70 independent hairy root lines, revealing strong editing activity for one guide, while the second proved non-functional despite favourable in silico predictions. Sequence analysis identified a range of insertions and deletions, including a homozygous biallelic mutant carrying frame-shifting deletions predicted to abolish transporter function. Using the validated guide RNA, stable Agrobacterium tumefaciens-mediated transformation yielded heritable mutations, and transgene-free knockout lines were recovered in subsequent generations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This study establishes a robust hairy root-based gRNA validation platform for M. truncatula, generates stable and heritable mtabcg46 knockout lines, and provides a scalable framework for functional characterization of ABCG transporters in specialized metabolism and plant defense.","affiliation":"Department of Plant Molecular Physiology, Institute of Bioorganic Chemistry, Polish Academy of Sciences, Z. Noskowskiego 12/14, Poznań, 61- 704, Poland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Narodowe Centrum Nauki","grantId":"2020/39/B/NZ9/00784","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Abc Transporters","Medicago Truncatula","Crispr/cas9","Phenylpropanoid Pathway"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13007-026-01582-x"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13729384"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13729384?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41979903","source":"MED","pmid":"41979903","pmcid":"PMC13185153","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13185153"]},"doi":"10.1021/acssynbio.5c00843","title":"PHYCUT: Scalable Multiplex CRISPR/Cas9 Editing for Genome Engineering in the Diatom &lt;i&gt;Phaeodactylum tricornutum&lt;/i&gt;.","authorString":"Stuckless EE, Gai LS, Slattery SS, Dempsey KH, Browne TS, Gloor GB, Edgell DR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Stuckless EE","lastName":"Stuckless","firstName":"Emily E","initials":"EE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Gai LS","lastName":"Gai","firstName":"Lian S","initials":"LS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Slattery SS","lastName":"Slattery","firstName":"Samuel S","initials":"SS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Dempsey KH","lastName":"Dempsey","firstName":"Kira H","initials":"KH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Browne TS","lastName":"Browne","firstName":"Tyler S","initials":"TS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7832-9867"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Gloor GB","lastName":"Gloor","firstName":"Gregory B","initials":"GB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5803-3380"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Edgell DR","lastName":"Edgell","firstName":"David R","initials":"DR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3858-6150"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5803-3380"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7832-9867"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3858-6150"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4194059,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"ACS Synth Biol","title":"ACS synthetic biology","essn":"2161-5063","issn":"2161-5063","nlmid":"101575075","isoabbreviation":"ACS Synth Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1850-1865","abstractText":"Diatoms are globally significant microalgae that contribute ∼20% of oxygen production and exhibit remarkable metabolic diversity. The marine diatom <i>Phaeodactylum tricornutum</i> has emerged as a promising synthetic biology platform for the bioproduction of recombinant proteins, supported by a human-like <i>N</i>-linked glycosylation pathway. However, its α(1,3)-linked core fucose is potentially immunogenic in humans and thus limits its biopharmaceutical applications. One hurdle to efficient genome engineering in <i>P. tricornutum</i> is the lack of a robust system for simultaneous CRISPR/Cas9 editing at multiple sites. To overcome this limitation, we develop PHYCUT (<i><b>Ph</b>aeodactylum tricornutum</i> Cs<b>y</b>4-<b>C</b>as9 m<b>u</b>ltiplex <b>t</b>ool), a versatile plasmid-based CRISPR/Cas9 system that uses the Csy4 endoribonuclease to process multiguide RNA arrays. To highlight PHYCUT applications, we demonstrate multiplex editing of all three <i>FucT</i> genes responsible for α(1,3) fucosylation in <i>P. tricornutum</i>, yielding strains with reduced fucosylation of secreted proteins. PHYCUT enables facile, multiplexed genome engineering in diatoms and provides a foundation for humanizing the <i>P. tricornutum</i> glycosylation pathway to support next-generation algal biotechnology.","affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Schulich School of Medicine & Dentistry, Western University, London ON N6A 5C1, Canada.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grantId":"RGPIN-06519-2025","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grantId":"RGPIN-2022-05459","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Government of Ontario","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grantId":"ALLRP 565307-2021","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Diatoms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Engineering","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glycosylation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plasmids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Phaeodactylum tricornutum","Fucosylation","N-linked Glycosylation","Crispr/cas9","Csy4","Fuct Multigene Family","Sgrna Array"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acssynbio.5c00843"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13185153"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13185153?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"PPR","id":"PPR1112242","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-21","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42050729","source":"MED","pmid":"42050729","pmcid":"PMC13453242","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13453242"]},"doi":"10.1002/ps.70864","title":"Resistance of Botrytis cinerea to anilinopyrimidine fungicides: A novel ARMS-PCR method for the detection of Bcpos5 mutations and characterization of resistance using CRISPR/Cas9 editing.","authorString":"Sofianos G, Petmezas A, Samaras A, Karaoglanidis G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sofianos G","lastName":"Sofianos","firstName":"Georgios","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Agriculture, Forestry and Natural Environment, Laboratory of Plant Pathology, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Petmezas A","lastName":"Petmezas","firstName":"Athanasios","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Agriculture, Forestry and Natural Environment, Laboratory of Plant Pathology, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Samaras A","lastName":"Samaras","firstName":"Anastasios","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Systemic Biology, Department of Organismal Biology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Karaoglanidis G","lastName":"Karaoglanidis","firstName":"George","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7413-2052"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Agriculture, Forestry and Natural Environment, Laboratory of Plant Pathology, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7413-2052"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"82","journalIssueId":4241643,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pest Manag Sci","title":"Pest management science","essn":"1526-4998","issn":"1526-498X","nlmid":"100898744","isoabbreviation":"Pest Manag Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"8189-8198","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Anilinopyrimidine (AP) fungicides have been widely used against Botrytis cinerea, yet their resistance mechanisms have only recently been clarified. Resistance is primarily linked to mutations G408V, L412V, and L412F in the Bcpos5 gene, whose encoded protein is localized to the mitochondria. In this study, we developed a detection method and tested the fitness of L412F/V mutants obtained by using the CRISPR/Cas9 editing technique.<h4>Results</h4>For rapid and cost-effective mutation identification, a TETRA-primer amplification refractory mutation system polymerase chain reaction (T-ARMS-PCR) was developed to rapidly detect the nucleotide alterations that lead to L412F and L412V mutations, producing a 702 bp band in all isolates, with additional 470 bp (F) or 252 bp (V) fragments. Isolates harboring only the 702 bp band were further digested with MlyI to confirm the mutation leading to the amino acid substitution G408V mutation (467 bp + 235 bp). Results were validated by Sanger sequencing. Application of the assay to 170 isolates from strawberry and tomato revealed mutation frequencies of 70.2% (L412F), 8.3% (L412V), and 4.7% (G408V) within the resistant fraction of the population (n = 82 resistant isolates). Furthermore, sequencing analysis revealed also a low frequency of the E407K mutation in Bcmdl1, along with evidence of additional, yet undefined, resistance mechanisms. To further characterize the mutations, the B. cinerea reference strain B05.10 was transformed with L412F and L412V alleles via CRISPR/Cas9 and homologous recombination. The resulting mutants displayed resistance to cyprodinil, and potential fitness costs were assessed in both field-derived and CRISPR/Cas9-generated isolates through measurements of mycelial growth and sporulation in vitro, and pathogenicity in planta. The L412F and L412V transformants did not differ significantly from the parental B05.10 strain in any of the evaluated fitness parameters.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Overall, the developed ARMS PCR offers a fast, cost-effective tool for resistance monitoring aiming to identify the most common mutations conferring resistance to APs, while CRISPR/Cas9 provides an efficient approach for functional validation of resistance mutations in B. cinerea. Using this approach, we confirmed that L412F and L412V mutations in Bcpos5 confer resistance to APs, while are not associated with fitness cost. © 2026 The Author(s). Pest Management Science published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of Society of Chemical Industry.","affiliation":"Faculty of Agriculture, Forestry and Natural Environment, Laboratory of Plant Pathology, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, Greece.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"General Secretariat for 