uthorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8060-2869"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4103312,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Insects","title":"Insects","essn":"2075-4450","issn":"2075-4450","nlmid":"101574235","isoabbreviation":"Insects"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-12-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"35","abstractText":"Globally, <i>Spodoptera frugiperda</i> is a major threat to many important crops, including maize, rice, and cotton, causing significant economic damage. To control this invasive pest, environmentally friendly pest control techniques, including pheromone detection and identification of potential molecular targets to disrupt <i>S. frugiperda</i> mating communication, are needed. Female moths biosynthesize pheromones and emit them from the pheromone gland, which significantly depends on the intrinsic factors of the moth. Male <i>S. frugiperda</i> have a sophisticated olfactory circuit on their antennae that recognizes pheromone blends via olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs). With its potential to significantly modify the insect genome, CRISPR/Cas9 offers a revolutionary strategy to control this insect pest. The impairing physiological behaviors and disrupting the <i>S. frugiperda</i> volatile-sensing mechanism are the main potential applications of CRISPR/Ca9 explored in this review. Furthermore, the release of mutant <i>S. frugiperda</i> for their long-term persistence must be integral to the adoption of this technology. Looking forward, CRISPR/Cas9-based gene drive systems have the potential to synergistically target pheromone signaling pathways in <i>S. frugiperda</i> by disrupting pheromone receptors and key biosynthesis genes, thereby effectively blocking intraspecific communication and reproductive success. In conclusion, CRISPR/Cas9 provides an environmentally friendly and revolutionary platform for precise, targeted pest management in <i>S. frugiperda</i>.","affiliation":"Agricultural College, Jilin Agricultural Science and Technology College, Jilin 132101, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Innovation Capacity Building Project of the Jilin Provincial Development and Reform Commission.","grantId":"2024C014-2","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sex pheromone","Olfactory system","Odorant receptors","Spodoptera Frugiperda","Crispr/cas System","Orco Knockout"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/insects17010035"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12842117"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12842117?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-25","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42071241","source":"MED","pmid":"42071241","pmcid":"PMC13137644","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13137644"]},"doi":"10.1186/s40104-026-01402-1","title":"Improvement of chicken genome editing efficiency in vitro using ribonucleoprotein-mediated CRISPR/Cas9 delivery.","authorString":"Han Y, Kwon CY, Han JY.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Han Y","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Yujin","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Major in Biomodulation, Animal Science and Biotechnology, Department of Agricultural Biotechnology and Research Institute of Agriculture and Life Sciences, College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, Seoul National University, Seoul, Republic of Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kwon CY","lastName":"Kwon","firstName":"Chan Young","initials":"CY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Major in Biomodulation, Animal Science and Biotechnology, Department of Agricultural Biotechnology and Research Institute of Agriculture and Life Sciences, College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, Seoul National University, Seoul, Republic of Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Han JY","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Jae Yong","initials":"JY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Major in Biomodulation, Animal Science and Biotechnology, Department of Agricultural Biotechnology and Research Institute of Agriculture and Life Sciences, College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, Seoul National University, Seoul, Republic of Korea. jaehan@snu.ac.kr."},{"affiliation":"Avinnogen Co., Ltd., Seoul, Republic of Korea. jaehan@snu.ac.kr."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4187170,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Anim Sci Biotechnol","title":"Journal of animal science and biotechnology","essn":"2049-1891","issn":"1674-9782","nlmid":"101581293","isoabbreviation":"J Anim Sci Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"82","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Limitations in genome editing of avian species, including chicken, due to the inaccessibility of one-cell zygotes, have led to the manipulation of primordial germ cells (PGCs) as the primary approach for generating genetically engineered birds. Although plasmid-mediated delivery of clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (CRISPR/Cas9) has been widely used, it has several limitations, including delayed nuclease activation, increased off-target effects, cytotoxicity, and the inability to apply in vivo selection strategies. Collectively, these limitations highlight the need to develop strategies that achieve high on-target activity at the initial editing step.<h4>Results</h4>In this study, we compared plasmid- and ribonucleoprotein (RNP)-mediated CRISPR/Cas9 delivery in Leghorn male hepatoma (LMH) cells and in PGCs targeting three loci: deleted in azoospermia like (DAZL), chicken vasa homologue (CVH), and stimulated by retinoic acid 8 (STRA8). RNP delivery showed comparable or higher cell viability and editing efficiency than plasmid delivery in both cell types. Insertion/deletion (indel) profiling revealed broader and more diverse mutation patterns with RNPs, consistent with a shift in repair pathway engagement toward microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ), which favors larger deletions. Off-target analysis further showed substantially reduced off-target editing and high specificity with RNP delivery.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Together, these findings demonstrate that RNP delivery improves efficiency, reduces cytotoxicity, and improves precision, providing a more reliable platform for chicken genetic engineering.","affiliation":"Major in Biomodulation, Animal Science and Biotechnology, Department of Agricultural Biotechnology and Research Institute of Agriculture and Life Sciences, College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, Seoul National University, Seoul, Republic of Korea.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Kore","grantId":"RS-2024-00418297","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Korea(NRF)","grantId":"RS-2024-00418297","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Chicken","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Ribonucleoprotein (Rnp)","Indel Profiling"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40104-026-01402-1"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13137644"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13137644?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42182968","source":"MED","pmid":"42182968","pmcid":"PMC13197704","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13197704"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.synbio.2026.04.015","title":"OmniEdit: A unified CRISPR/Cas9 platform for precise genome engineering and strain optimization of the &lt;i&gt;Cordyceps militaris&lt;/i&gt; cell factory.","authorString":"Liu M, Chen S, Wang J, Meng G, Dong C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu M","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Mengqian","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen S","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Sijin","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Zunyi Medical University, Zunyi, 563000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang J","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Jingting","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China."},{"affiliation":"Shandong Provincial Key Laboratory of Applied Mycology, College of Life Sciences, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Meng G","lastName":"Meng","firstName":"Guoliang","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dong C","lastName":"Dong","firstName":"Caihong","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4182072,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Synth Syst Biotechnol","title":"Synthetic and systems biotechnology","essn":"2405-805X","issn":"2405-805X","nlmid":"101694371","isoabbreviation":"Synth Syst Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"255-266","abstractText":"<i>Cordyceps militaris</i> serves as a critical microbial cell factory for high-value bioactive compounds; however, the scarcity of versatile and sophisticated genome-editing toolsets significantly restricts its systematic metabolic engineering. This study aimed to develop OmniEdit, a unified and highly efficient CRISPR/Cas9-based platform, to streamline diverse and complex genetic modifications for strain engineering. We integrated multiple editing modalities into a standardized workflow utilizing an AMA1-based CRISPR/Cas9 system combined with customized homologous donor templates. The platform's robustness was systematically validated through five distinct engineering tasks delivered via PEG-mediated transformation, including precise point mutation, <i>in situ</i> fluorescent protein tagging, large biosynthetic gene cluster (BGC) deletion, regulatory upstream open reading frame (uORF) disruption, and one-step multiplexed gene knockout. OmniEdit achieved the first CRISPR-based precise point mutation (∼4-5% efficiency) in this fungus. It enabled the surgical excision of an entire ∼26 kb BGC (∼10% efficiency) and efficient one-step dual-gene knockout (∼30% efficiency). Furthermore, targeted uORF editing was implemented to modulate translational efficiency, alongside flexible protein tagging for subcellular analysis. By overcoming technical bottlenecks, OmniEdit provides a standardized, powerful toolkit for functional genomics and the systematic enhancement of <i>C. militaris</i> as a high-performance bioproduction platform.","affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Microbial Diversity and Innovative Utilization, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"32272786","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Point mutation","Large Fragment Deletion","Fluorescent Protein Tagging","One-step Dual-gene Knockout","Uorf Editing"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.synbio.2026.04.015"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197704"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197704?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1244145","source":"PPR","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PPR1244145"]},"doi":"10.64898/2026.06.01.729453","title":"Phage-encoded single-guide RNAs subvert CRISPR-Cas9 immunity","authorString":"Feussner M, Migur A, Li J, Riesenberg S, Birkholz N, Mayo-Muñoz D, Wakelin H, Brown CM, Weinberg Z, Pinilla-Redondo R, Fineran PC, Beisel CL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Feussner M","lastName":"Feussner","firstName":"Maximilian","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bioinformatics Group, Department of Computer Science and Interdisciplinary Centre for Bioinformatics, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Migur A","lastName":"Migur","firstName":"Angela","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Botnar Institute of Immune Engineering, Basel, Switzerland"}]}},{"fullName":"Li J","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Jinquan","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Riesenberg S","lastName":"Riesenberg","firstName":"Stephan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":", ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Birkholz N","lastName":"Birkholz","firstName":"Nils","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Mayo-Muñoz D","lastName":"Mayo-Muñoz","firstName":"David","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Wakelin H","lastName":"Wakelin","firstName":"Holly","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Brown CM","lastName":"Brown","firstName":"Chris","initials":"CM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0079-7067"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Weinberg Z","lastName":"Weinberg","firstName":"Zasha","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bioinformatics Group, Department of Computer Science and Interdisciplinary Centre for Bioinformatics, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Pinilla-Redondo R","lastName":"Pinilla-Redondo","firstName":"Rafael","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3521-460X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Section of Molecular Microbiology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Fineran PC","lastName":"Fineran","firstName":"Peter","initials":"PC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Beisel CL","lastName":"Beisel","firstName":"Chase","initials":"CL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Botnar Institute of Immune Engineering, Basel, Switzerland"}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0079-7067"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3521-460X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>ABSTRACT</h4>  Central to CRISPR technologies is the single-guide RNA (sgRNA), an engineered fusion of a processed CRISPR RNA and tracrRNA that directs Cas9 and many Cas12 nucleases to bind and cleave target DNA  1–4 . Here, we report the discovery of similarly compact viral sgRNAs (vsgRNAs) encoded by bacteriophages that counteract bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 immunity. vsgRNAs inhibit Cas9 function via two complementary routes: by sequestering the Cas9 apoenzyme, and by re-directing the Cas9 nuclease to transcriptionally silence its own promoter. vsgRNAs also can cooperate with co-encoded anti-CRISPR proteins (Acrs), including AcrIIA25.1 that blocks DNA binding by Cas9 complexed with a standard sgRNA but not with the vsgRNA. We predict that phages evolved vsgRNAs by co-opting and repurposing host-encoded long-form tracrRNAs (tracr-L) responsible for Cas9 auto-repression and a countermeasure to Acrs  5,6 . Our search also uncovered Cas9-regulating small CRISPR-associated RNAs (scaRNAs), which we predict were inserted upstream of tracrRNAs to form tracr-L but were also co-opted by phages as viral scaRNAs to suppress Cas9 immunity. Finally, we found that vsgRNAs can enable genome editing in mammalian cells, offering a natural guide RNA template for CRISPR technologies. Overall, these findings reveal that bacteriophages devised their own compact sgRNAs tailored to subvert Cas9 immunity, long preceding their rational design to program RNA-guided nucleases  2 .","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"European Research Council","grantId":"865973","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.01.729453"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/article/PPR/PPR1244145"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"Y","nihAuthMan":"N","manuscriptId":"EMS219162","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41909467","source":"MED","pmid":"41909467","pmcid":"PMC13022685","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022685"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.omtn.2026.102896","title":"Exosome-like nanovesicles from acerola for CRISPR-Cas9 ribonucleoprotein delivery to the central nervous system.","authorString":"Nagamatsu Y, Umezu T, Hong T, Niijima T, Ohno SI, Harada Y, Kanekura K, Ochiya T, Kuroda M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nagamatsu Y","lastName":"Nagamatsu","firstName":"Yui","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Umezu T","lastName":"Umezu","firstName":"Tomohiro","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hong T","lastName":"Hong","firstName":"Taehun","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2947-1896"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Niijima T","lastName":"Niijima","firstName":"Takahide","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ohno SI","lastName":"Ohno","firstName":"Shin-Ichiro","initials":"SI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Harada Y","lastName":"Harada","firstName":"Yuichiro","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kanekura K","lastName":"Kanekura","firstName":"Kohsuke","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ochiya T","lastName":"Ochiya","firstName":"Takahiro","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1064-6531"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular and Cellular Medicine, Institute of Medical Science, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kuroda M","lastName":"Kuroda","firstName":"Masahiko","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7052-4289"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7052-4289"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2947-1896"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1064-6531"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4156482,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Ther Nucleic Acids","title":"Molecular therapy. Nucleic acids","essn":"2162-2531","issn":"2162-2531","nlmid":"101581621","isoabbreviation":"Mol Ther Nucleic Acids"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"102896","abstractText":"An aberrant six-base repeat in intron 1 of <i>C9orf72</i> is the most frequent cause of solitary and familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia. This mutation is a potential target for CRISPR/Cas9-based genome editing. However, the blood-brain barrier and limitations of current viral or nanoparticle-based delivery systems to neurons significantly restrict the clinical application of CRISPR-Cas9 in the brain. To address these challenges, we developed a drug delivery system using acerola-derived exosome-like nanoparticles (AELNs), which may overcome several limitations associated with human exosomes. AELNs stably form complexes with ribonucleoproteins (RNPs) comprised of Cas9 proteins and guide RNAs (gRNAs). We improved the delivery efficiency and selectivity of AELN/RNP complexes in GLP2-receptor-expressing neurons by incorporating GLP2 peptides into the AELN/RNP complexes. Intranasal administration of peptide-tagged AELN/RNP complexes <i>in vivo</i> confirmed the successful genome editing of <i>C9orf72</i>, demonstrating the potential of this system for treating neurodegenerative diseases. This study presents a potentially innovative approach for <i>in vivo</i> genome editing using a noninvasive delivery system.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Pathology, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-arti