2873579"]},"doi":"10.17912/micropub.biology.001974","title":"Epigenetic silencing is a barrier to editing the X chromosome using the SEC-based CRISPR/Cas9 knock-in protocol in &lt;i&gt;C. elegans&lt;/i&gt;.","authorString":"Iyer R, Ferreria S, Guvvala L, Borden H, Arora A, Shirsat R, Doonan R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Iyer R","lastName":"Iyer","firstName":"Ryka","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Stuart Country Day School, Princeton, New Jersey, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Ferreria S","lastName":"Ferreria","firstName":"Simon","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Livingston High School, Livingston, New Jersey, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Guvvala L","lastName":"Guvvala","firstName":"Laahya","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Downingtown S.T.E.M. Academy, Downingtown, Pennsylvania, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Borden H","lastName":"Borden","firstName":"Hugh","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Mercer Island High School, Mercer Island, Washington, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Arora A","lastName":"Arora","firstName":"Aariv","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Stargate School, Thornton, Colorado, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Shirsat R","lastName":"Shirsat","firstName":"Rebecca","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Austin Community College, Austin, Texas, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Doonan R","lastName":"Doonan","firstName":"Ryan","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Austin Community College, Austin, Texas, United States."},{"affiliation":"Eureka Program, Biology and Chemistry Division, New York, New York, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2026","journalIssueId":4098428,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"MicroPubl Biol","title":"microPublication biology","essn":"2578-9430","issn":"2578-9430","nlmid":"101759238","isoabbreviation":"MicroPubl Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Self-excising-cassette (SEC)-based CRISPR/Cas9 knock-in is widely used for generating endogenous fluorescent protein tags in <i>C. elegans</i> . Here, we report a lack of success targeting the X chromosome using this method. CRISPR/Cas9 works as intended, but subsequent floxing of the SEC is blocked. Given that the X chromosome is epigenetically silenced in primordial germ cells (PGCs), this is a logical result. To circumvent this barrier, we suppressed polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) with RNAi to transiently and reversibly reduce silencing in the PGCs, creating a brief window where the X chromosome is amenable to floxing without compromising germ line development. Overall, our results reveal a previously unrecognized limitation of SEC-based CRISPR/Cas9 knock-in and identify a reliable workaround for tagging proteins encoded on the X chromosome.","affiliation":"High School CRISPR Academy, Austin, Texas, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.17912/micropub.biology.001974"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12873579"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12873579?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42177737","source":"MED","pmid":"42177737","pmcid":"PMC13376088","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13376088"]},"doi":"10.1007/s12672-026-05272-x","title":"PSMB1 screened by CRISPR-CAS9 promoted breast cancer progression via PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling.","authorString":"Chen LW, Ning J, Ye YQ, Ye HB, Zeng H, Wang LJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen LW","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Lu-Wen","initials":"LW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Ganzhou Cancer Hospital, Ganzhou, 341000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ning J","lastName":"Ning","firstName":"Jing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Hainan Branch, School of Medicine, Shanghai Children' s Medical Center, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Hainan, 57000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye YQ","lastName":"Ye","firstName":"Yong-Qiang","initials":"YQ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Ganzhou Cancer Hospital, Ganzhou, 341000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye HB","lastName":"Ye","firstName":"Hua-Bin","initials":"HB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Ganzhou Cancer Hospital, Ganzhou, 341000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zeng H","lastName":"Zeng","firstName":"Hui","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Ganzhou Cancer Hospital, Ganzhou, 341000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang LJ","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Li-Juan","initials":"LJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Hainan Branch, School of Medicine, Shanghai Children' s Medical Center, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Hainan, 57000, China. mclendon@hhu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4190649,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Discov Oncol","title":"Discover oncology","essn":"2730-6011","issn":"2730-6011","nlmid":"101775142","isoabbreviation":"Discov Oncol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1039","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Breast cancer is a threat to women's health worldwide. Recent studies have focused on the role of differentially expressed genes in tumors. PSMB1 has been reported to play an important role in tumors, however, its role in breast cancer has not been well defined.<h4>Methods</h4>The CRISPR-CAS9 data was analyzed and intersected with Cox univariate analyzation to screen differentially expressed genes in breast cancer. The prognosis of PSMB1 was predicted by Kaplan-Meier plotter, and the related major pathways were analyzed through GO analysis and GSEA. PSMB1-related gene mutations and Tumor microenvironment characteristics were analyzed. The expression of PSMB1 in breast cancer tissues and cells were determined by RT-qPCR and IHC. MTT, colony formation assay, EdU, Transwell, wound healing assay and cell cycle assay were performed to observe its function in vitro. The western blot was conducted to detect its regulation on EMT markers and key proteins in PI3K pathway.<h4>Results</h4>In this study, PSMB1 was screened by CRISPR-Cas9 and identified to be upregulated in breast cancer samples. By further analysis, PSMB1 was found to be involved in tumor-related biological process and correlated with worse prognosis in breast cancer patients. In vitro experiments verified that downregulation of PSMB1 could inhibit the malignant phenotype of breast cancer cells. The western blot showed that si-PSMB1 significantly decreased the expression of key proteins and their phosphorylation form in PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling.<h4>Conclusion</h4>si-PSMB1 inhibited breast cancer cell proliferation, invasion and migration probably by targeting PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling, which brings innovative mechanisms for breast cancer regulation and emerging ideas for therapeutic strategies.","affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, Ganzhou Cancer Hospital, Ganzhou, 341000, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Sanya University and medical institutions special science and technology project","grantId":"2021GXYL30","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Breast cancer","Crispr-cas9","Pi3k-akt-mtor Signaling","Psmb1"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12672-026-05272-x"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13376088"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle"