Instituto de Ciências Básicas da Saúde, R. Sarmento Leite, 500, Porto Alegre 90035-190, RS, Brazil."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8401-3332"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9655-3686"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9330-0154"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0554-0295"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1598-5861"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4189-0436"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4977-6662"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-2820-2814"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4161799,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pharmaceutics","title":"Pharmaceutics","essn":"1999-4923","issn":"1999-4923","nlmid":"101534003","isoabbreviation":"Pharmaceutics"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"281","abstractText":"<b>Background/Objectives</b>: Mucopolysaccharidosis type I (MPS I) is a lysosomal storage disorder caused by α-L-iduronidase (IDUA) deficiency, leading to progressive glycosaminoglycan (GAG) accumulation and severe joint involvement. Gene editing represents a promising alternative to restore localized enzyme production. Therefore, this study aimed to evaluate the feasibility, efficacy, and safety of in situ genome editing through intra-articular administration of a nonviral CRISPR/Cas9 system to increase localized IDUA expression in an MPS I mouse model. <b>Methods</b>: Cationic liposomes were formulated to deliver plasmids encoding the CRISPR/Cas9 system targeted to the <i>ROSA26</i> locus along with an <i>IDUA</i> donor sequence. In vitro assays were performed in fibroblast-like synoviocytes (FLSs) isolated from MPS I mice to assess cytotoxicity, gene editing efficiency, and IDUA activity. In vivo, MPS I mice received intra-articular injections in the knee joints, either as a single dose (short-term study) or monthly for three months (long-term study). IDUA activity, GAG levels, and genome editing efficiency were evaluated in joint tissues, synovial fluid, serum, and major organs. <b>Results</b>: Gene-edited FLS showed sustained IDUA activity for up to 30 days with low cytotoxicity. In vivo, intra-articular administration resulted in a significant increase in IDUA activity in joint tissue and synovial fluid without detectable systemic IDUA. Long-term treatment led to persistent joint-localized IDUA activity, significant reductions (>50%) in GAG levels, and detectable genome editing in joint DNA. <b>Conclusions</b>: Intra-articular delivery of CRISPR/Cas9 via cationic liposomes enables safe and effective localized genome editing, representing a promising strategy for treating joint manifestations of MPS I.","affiliation":"Laboratório Células, Tecidos e Genes do Hospital de Clínicas de Porto Alegre, R. Ramiro Barcelos, 2350, Porto Alegre 90035-903, RS, Brazil.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Financiamento e Incentivo à Pesquisa of Hospital de Clínicas de Porto Alegre FIPE/HCPA","grantId":"2019-0122","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development (CNPq)","grantId":"470888/2014-8 and 141742/2014-3","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Gene therapy","Liposome","Microfluidics","Intra-articular","Mucopolysaccharidosis Type I","Nonviral Vector","Crispr/cas9"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18030281"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029379"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029379?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42112836","source":"MED","pmid":"42112836","pmcid":"PMC13228013","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13228013"]},"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.03899-25","title":"CRISPR-Cas9-mediated targeted gene deletion in &lt;i&gt;Aspergillus calidoustus&lt;/i&gt;, a non-model environmental mold.","authorString":"Hollomon JM, Dahlstrom KM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hollomon JM","lastName":"Hollomon","firstName":"Jeffrey M","initials":"JM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, Franklin College of Arts and Sciences, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dahlstrom KM","lastName":"Dahlstrom","firstName":"Kurt M","initials":"KM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6590-6020"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, Franklin College of Arts and Sciences, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6590-6020"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4208193,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Microbiol Spectr","title":"Microbiology spectrum","essn":"2165-0497","issn":"2165-0497","nlmid":"101634614","isoabbreviation":"Microbiol Spectr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0389925","abstractText":"Reverse genetic approaches in molds are complicated by their recalcitrance to transformation, multicellular growth during much of the vegetative life cycle, and frequent off-target integration of deletion constructs. Thus, protocols for gene deletion in filamentous fungi are predominantly confined to established model organisms. Adapting an existing protocol developed for gene deletion in the related fungus <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i>, we employed an expression-free CRISPR/Cas9 directed mutagenesis strategy to the non-model environmental mold <i>Aspergillus calidoustus</i>. As a test case, we have deleted <i>A. calidoustus pyrG</i>, encoding orotidine-5'-phosphate decarboxylase, using short regions of homology to guide on-target integration of a nourseothricin resistance cassette (NatR) to CRISPR/Cas9-induced double strand breaks. We genotypically and phenotypically validated two <i>A. calidoustus</i> Δ<i>pyrG</i> deletion strains generated using this methodology, with whole-genome sequencing revealing one Δ<i>pyrG</i> strain to have integrated the resistance cassette by homologous recombination, and another strain by non-homologous end joining. Thus, distinct modes of double-strand break repair were responsible for on-target integration of the homology-bearing NatR cassette at the <i>pyrG</i> locus in <i>A. calidoustus</i>.IMPORTANCEIn the environment, filamentous fungi play essential roles in soil health and agriculture, decomposition, and nutrient cycling. The study of these organisms is often limited by an inability to dissect the functions of their genes and the roles that they play by modifying the genomes of these organisms. Here, we adapt and validate a tool for genome modification to <i>Aspergillus calidoustus</i>, a soil fungus that forms a partnership with a bacterium (<i>Paraburkholderia edwinii</i>) to resist natural toxins found in the soil.","affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, Franklin College of Arts and Sciences, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"5R35GM150797","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grantId":"5R35GM150797","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Aspergillus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Orotidine-5'-Phosphate Decarboxylase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fungal Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Deletion"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Non-model Organism","Crispr-cas9","Environmental Isolate","Aspergillus Calidoustus"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Orotidine-5'-Phosphate Decarboxylase","registryNumber":"EC 4.1.1.23"},{"name":"Fungal Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.03899-25"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13228013"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":