"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0122-8038"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"105","journalIssueId":4300478,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Poult Sci","title":"Poultry science","essn":"1525-3171","issn":"0032-5791","nlmid":"0401150","isoabbreviation":"Poult Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"107469","abstractText":"Primordial germ cells (PGCs) are a unique platform for heritable gene editing in avian species, because they allow easy isolation, culture, and can then be reintroduced into the host. CRISPR/Cas9 technologies have advanced avian genome editing by enabling targeted editing of genes and traits for production, health, reproduction, and welfare. This review critically evaluates genome-editing tools in avian PGCs, including CRISPR/Cas9, transcription activator-like effector nucleases (TALENs), base editing, and prime editing. Germline transmission efficiency, suitability, precision, and heritability are compared. Efficiency, cytotoxicity, and translational feasibility of delivery strategies, including viral vectors, electroporation and the use of ribonucleoproteins are assessed. Applications of these techniques in chickens are for muscle growth via myostatin (MSTN) gene disruption, viral resistance via editing of the sodium/hydrogen exchanger 1 (NHE1) gene, and control of male and female ratios through modification of sex determination genes. Additionally, applications in biopharmaceutical protein production and animal biodiversity conservation are also explored. Despite advances, some limitations remain, including low efficiency of homology-directed repair, off-target effects, mosaicism, and variability in transmission through the germ line. Current evidence demonstrates a significant lack of germline validation and scalability creating barriers to translate this potential into commercial avian production, especially, poultry breeding. Regulatory frameworks and their implications for food and commercialization are also discussed. Future research should prioritize precision editing, scalable delivery systems, and regulatory alignment to enable practical, ethical, and responsible implementation.","affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou- 225009,5 Jiangsu, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Germ Cells"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Chickens","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Germline transmission","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Avian Pgcs","Poultry Biotechnology"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psj.2026.107469"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13486796"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13486796?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-11","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42170672","source":"MED","pmid":"42170672","pmcid":"PMC13187952","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13187952"]},"doi":"10.17912/micropub.biology.002076","title":"Development and Optimization of CRISPR/Cas9-Assisted Recombineering in &lt;i&gt;Escherichia albertii&lt;/i&gt;.","authorString":"Khan SA, Miller TM, Bhatt S, Li E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khan SA","lastName":"Khan","firstName":"Shahab Ahmad","initials":"SA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Saint Joseph's University, Philadelphia, PA, US."}]}},{"fullName":"Miller TM","lastName":"Miller","firstName":"Tara Marie","initials":"TM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Saint Joseph's University, Philadelphia, PA, US."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhatt S","lastName":"Bhatt","firstName":"Shantanu","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Saint Joseph's University, Philadelphia, PA, US."}]}},{"fullName":"Li E","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Edwin","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Saint Joseph's University, Philadelphia, PA, US."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2026","journalIssueId":4098428,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"MicroPubl Biol","title":"microPublication biology","essn":"2578-9430","issn":"2578-9430","nlmid":"101759238","isoabbreviation":"MicroPubl Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<i>Escherichia albertii</i> is a zoonotic pathogen frequently misidentified as diarrheagenic <i>E. coli</i> due to shared phenotypic and genetic traits, yet functional genomic studies in this species have been limited by the inefficiency of traditional genetic tools. To address this, we developed a bipartite CRISPR/Cas9-assisted lambda red (\uD835\uDECC-Red) recombineering system for efficient, markerless genome editing in <i>E. albertii</i> . As proof of principle, we targeted the nonessential <i>lacZ</i> gene for negative selection by using Cas9 to generate lethal double stranded breaks in unedited cells. Using a recombinogenic single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) oligonucleotide to introduce a premature stop codon and an <i>NheI</i> restriction site, we achieved a recombination efficiency of 53%. Extending the induction time of the \uD835\uDECC-Red recombinase genes enhanced recombineering efficiency to 80%. This optimized CRISPR-assisted platform, the first reported application of its kind in <i>E. albertii,</i> enables the rapid generation of scarless mutations and provides a robust tool for the systematic analysis of <i>E. albertii</i> virulence factors and regulatory networks.","affiliation":"Department of Biology, Saint Joseph's University, Philadelphia, PA, US.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.17912/micropub.biology.002076"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13187952"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13187952?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42212195","reference":"MicroPubl Biol. 2026 May 26;2026. doi: 10.17912/micropub.biology.002214.","type":"Erratum in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40995763","source":"MED","pmid":"40995763","pmcid":"PMC12461677","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12461677"]},"doi":"10.1002/cpz1.70210","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-Mediated Gene Knockout in Cereal Crops.","authorString":"Pramanik D, Wang K, Lee K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pramanik D","lastName":"Pramanik","firstName":"Dibyajyoti","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7996-1124"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agronomy, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."},{"affiliation":"Crop Bioengineering Center, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang K","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Kan","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2474-5800"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agronomy, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."},{"affiliation":"Crop Bioengineering Center, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee K","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Keunsub","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3174-2475"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agronomy, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."},{"affiliation":"Crop Bioengineering Center, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3174-2475"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7996-1124"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2474-5800"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"5","journalIssueId":4009335,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Protoc","title":"Current protocols","essn":"2691-1299","issn":"2691-1299","nlmid":"101773894","isoabbreviation":"Curr Protoc"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-09-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e70210","abstractText":"High-precision genome editing tools, such as programmable nucleases, are poised to transform crop breeding and significantly impact fundamental plant research. Among these tools, the CRISPR (Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats)/Cas9 (CRISPR-associated 9) system is a programmable, RNA-guided nuclease that introduces targeted, site-specific double-stranded breaks in the target DNA loci. When these breaks are repaired, it often results in a frame-shift mutation via short insertion/deletion (indel), leading to gene knockout. Since its first successful use in plants, CRISPR/Cas9 has been widely adopted for targeting genes of agronomic and scientific importance in multiple crops, including rice, maize, wheat, and sorghum. These cereal crops ensure global food security, provide essential nutrition, and support economic stability. Additionally, such crops support biofuel production, livestock feed, and sustainable farming practices through crop rotation. This article outlines the strategies for implementing CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in plants, including a step-by-step process of guide RNA target selection, oligonucleotide design, construct development, assembly, and analysis of genome edits. © 2025 The Author(s). Current Protocols published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. Basic Protocol 1: CRISPR/Cas9 guide RNA target selection Support Protocol 1: Genomic DNA extraction in-house protocol Basic Protocol 2: Construction of a binary plasmid vector Support Protocol 2: Agrobacterium transformation with a binary vector construct and stability check Support Protocol 3: Plant transformation Basic Protocol 3: Genotyping of edited events.","affiliation":"Department of Agronomy, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plants, Genetically Modified","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Crops, Agricultural","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome, Plant"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Knockout Techniques","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Edible Grain","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Rice","Maize","Sorghum","Cereal crops","Indels","Crispr","Genome Editing"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/cpz1.70210"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12461677"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12461677?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-25","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-25","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42036673","source":"MED","pmid":"42036673","pmcid":"PMC13273998","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13273998"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12951-026-04480-5","title":"MXene-based CRISPR/Cas9 nanoplatform targeting FABP5 for ROS amplification and synergistic photothermal/photodynamic therapy of cervical cancer.","authorString":"Jiang CQ, Song Z, Yan ZC, Liu Y, Zhang JJ, Yue B, Qiu M, Hu YJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jiang CQ","lastName":"Jiang","firstName":"Chang-Qing","initials":"CQ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical School of Obstetrics and Gynecology Center, Tianjin Medical University, Tianjin, 300100, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology, The Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, Qingdao, 266000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Song Z","lastName":"Song","firstName":"Zhen","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Marine Chemistry Theory and Technology, College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Ministry of Education, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan ZC","lastName":"Yan","firstName":"Zi-Chao","initials":"ZC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Marine Chemistry Theory and Technology, College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Ministry of Education, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Youchang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Qingdao Institute for Food and Drug Control, Qingdao, 266100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang JJ","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Jing-Jing","initials":"JJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology, The Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, Qingdao, 266000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yue B","lastName":"Yue","firstName":"Bin","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Oncology, The Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, Qingdao, 266000, China. bonetumoryb@qdu.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Qiu M","lastName":"Qiu","firstName":"Meng","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Marine Chemistry Theory and Technology, College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Ministry of Education, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266100, China. mengqiu@ouc.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu YJ","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Yuan-Jing","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology Oncology, Tianjin Central Hospital of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Tianjin, 300100, China. julianna_hu@163.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"24","journalIssueId":4170474,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Nanobiotechnology","title":"Journal of nanobiotechnology","essn":"1477-3155","issn":"1477-3155","nlmid":"101152208","isoabbreviation":"J Nanobiotechnology"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"560","abstractText":"Cervical cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality among women worldwide, underscoring the need for more effective therapeutic strategies. Photodynamic therapy (PDT) has gained attention in tumor treatment owing to its high selectivity and minimal invasiveness. However, PDT is often compromised by the intrinsic antioxidant defense systems of cervical cancer cells. Herein, we developed a gene editing photonic nanoplatform, MXene@PEI-FABP5 (MPF), which integrates the photothermal/photodynamic properties of MXene with CRISPR/Cas9-mediated FABP5 gene editing to achieve synergistically enhanced antitumor effects. Fatty acid binding protein 5 (FABP5), highly expressed in cervical cancer, plays a pivotal role in regulating lipid peroxidation and oxidative stress tolerance. By delivering the CRISPR/Cas9 system using MXene into tumor cells, FABP5 expression was effectively silenced, thereby disrupting cellular antioxidant defenses at the genetic level. Meanwhile, under 808 nm laser irradiation, MXene generated robust hyperthermia and reactive oxygen species (ROS), jointly amplifying oxidative stress and inducing cell death predominantly through apoptosis. Both in vitro and in vivo results demonstrated that MPF achieved an impressive tumor inhibition rate of ~96% while maintaining excellent biosafety. This work presents a \"gene editing and photothermal/photodynamic\" hybrid therapeutic paradigm, offering a promising avenue to overcome the limitations of conventional PDT and improve cervical cancer treatment outcomes.","affiliation":"Clinical School of Obstetrics and Gynecology Center, Tianjin Medical University, Tianjin, 300100, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities","grantId":"202341006","orderIn":0},{"agency":"the Young Scientists Fund of the National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"62405299","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Youth Foundation of Shandong Natural Science Foundation","grantId":"ZR2024QE315","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"62375249","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Tianjin Key Medicine Discipline (Specialty) Construction Project","grantId":"TJYXZDXK-3-029C","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Natural Science Foundation of Shandong Province","grantId":"ZR2022JQ22","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Taishan Scholar Project","grantId":"tsqn201909054","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nitrites"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transition Elements"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Reactive Oxygen Species","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Photochemotherapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Apoptosis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierNam