 Genetic Medicine, Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, Baltimore, MD."},{"affiliation":"Merkin Institute for Transformative Technologies in Healthcare, Broad Institute of Massachusetts Institute of Technology and Harvard, Cambridge, MA."},{"affiliation":"Institute for NanoBioTechnology, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD."}]}},{"fullName":"Tisdale JF","lastName":"Tisdale","firstName":"John F","initials":"JF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cellular and Molecular Therapeutics Branch, National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute/National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD."}]}},{"fullName":"Demirci S","lastName":"Demirci","firstName":"Selami","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6174-1609"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cellular and Molecular Therapeutics Branch, National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute/National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6322-8991"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7869-2615"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1074-8480"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3706-6615"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6174-1609"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9244-5407"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-6419-5762"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-1730-3502"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-6967-545X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1683-7739"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"10","journalIssueId":4091595,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Blood Adv","title":"Blood advances","essn":"2473-9537","issn":"2473-9529","nlmid":"101698425","isoabbreviation":"Blood Adv"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"289-300","abstractText":"<h4>Abstract</h4>Sickle cell disease (SCD) is a red blood cell disorder caused by a mutation in the β-globin gene, leading to sickle hemoglobin polymerization under low oxygen conditions. Both CRISPR-Cas9 editing and lentiviral transduction have shown promising clinical outcomes, but it remains unclear which approach is superior. Alternatively, new editing tools such as base editing may also be promising and reduce risks of genotoxicity. To compare these approaches, we studied them in an immunocompromised mouse model. We optimized ex vivo conditions in CD34+ hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPC) and infused edited SCD HSPC into busulfan-conditioned nonirradiated NOD,B6.SCID Il2rγ-/-KitW41/W41 (NBSGW) mice. Ex vivo analysis confirmed successful editing and transduction. At 16 weeks, bone marrow analysis showed similar human CD45+ cell engraftment across all groups (75%-90%). In the competitive transplantation group, there was a lower amount of B-cell lymphoma/leukemia 11A enhancer editing than base editing and lentiviral transduction. A secondary transplantation model yielded similar results. An antisickling assay showed significantly higher red blood cell sickling reduction in the base editing, transduction, and competitive transplantation groups compared to CRISPR-Cas9. In conclusion, although all methods showed therapeutic potential, base editing and lentiviral transduction provided superior outcomes over CRISPR-Cas9-mediated editing in a competitive murine transplantation model.","affiliation":"Cellular and Molecular Therapeutics Branch, National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute/National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hematopoietic Stem Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},